    <div class="green section_head">
      <h2>The Lives</h2>
      <h3>Biographies of the Objectivist Poets</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
    <p>The "Objectivists" were an assemblage of English-language poets whose writing was gathered and presented by Louis Zukofsky in two "Objectivist" publications: the February 1931 issue of <em>Poetry</em> magazine, and <em>An "Objectivists" Anthology</em>, published the following year in France. Beginning in the mid-1930s, most of the writers identified as "Objectivist" faded into obscurity until the early 1960s, when several members of the group reemerged as active poets and enjoyed a surge of attention and retroactive identification as "Objectivists." The seven "Objectivist" writers featured on this website: Louis Zukofsky, Charles Reznikoff, George Oppen, William Carlos Williams, Carl Rakosi, Basil Bunting and Lorine Niedecker were connected through a shifting web of friendship and joint publication beginning in the mid-1920s and stretching into the early twenty-first century. They were also loosely united by shared political and poetic affinities: each was leftist in their politics, with several possessing strong Marxist or socialist sympathies, and their poetics might be described as sympathetically heterogeneous, with Ezra Pound and the imagist tradition serving as important touchstones for the group.</p>

    <p>Speaking purely in terms of the lives of these "Objectivists," five of the seven were of the same 'generation,' having been born between 1900 and 1908. The two exceptions were William Carlos Williams, born in Rutherford, New Jersey on September 17, 1883, and Charles Reznikoff, who was born in New York City on August 31, 1894. Of the five with 21th century birthdates, Bunting was the eldest, having been born on March 1, 1900 in Scotswood-on-Tyne, Northumberland, England and Oppen was the youngest, having been born on April 24, 1908 in New Rochelle, New York. Less than a year separated Niedecker, Rakosi, and Zukofsky: Niedecker was born on May 12, 1903 on Blackhawk Island near Fort Atkinson, Wisconsin; Carl Rakosi was born on November 6, 1903 in Berlin, Germany; and Louis Zukofsky was born on January 23, 1904 in New York City. Not surprisingly, considering his seniority relative to the rest of the group, Williams was also the first of the "Objectivists" to die, in 1963, just as his fellow "Objectivists" were beginning to resurface on the American literary scene. Rakosi, the last surviving member of the group, published his final volume of poetry in 1999 and continued to publish new work in magazines until shortly before his death on June 25, 2004.</p>

